window.__finlexDocLoaded(10211,{"id":10211,"articles":[{"id":1000880,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Core Objective (Public Summary)","text":"Governs classification of debt (callable/refinanced obligations), simplified convertible debt accounting without cash-conversion/CCF bifurcation (ASC 470-20, ASU 2020-06), and debt modifications/extinguishments (ASC 470-50, 10% cash-flow test).","seq":0},{"id":1000881,"no":"ASC 470-20","heading":"Convertible Debt Single-Liability Model (ASU 2020-06) & Debt Classification","text":"• Simplified Convertible Debt (ASU 2020-06, effective FY2022+ for PBEs): Eliminated the Cash Conversion Feature (CCF) and Beneficial Conversion Feature (BCF) separation models. Most traditional convertible debt is now accounted for as a SINGLE LIABILITY at amortized cost (no equity bifurcation and no non-cash interest accretion from debt discounts), unless the conversion option must be bifurcated as an embedded derivative (ASC 815) or issued at a substantial premium.\n• EPS Impact (ASC 260): Mandatory 'if-converted' method (eliminating treasury stock method for net-share-settled convertibles where principal may be settled in shares).\n• Covenant Violations & Refinancing (ASC 470-10-45): Short-term debt refinanced on a long-term basis AFTER balance sheet date but BEFORE financial statement issuance qualifies as NONCURRENT (contrast with IAS 1, which requires refinancing agreement before balance sheet date).","seq":1},{"id":1000882,"no":"ASC 470-50","heading":"Debt Modifications vs. Extinguishments (10% Cash Flow Test)","text":"• 10% Present-Value Test (ASC 470-50-40-10): Compare present value of modified cash flows to remaining original cash flows discounted at the ORIGINAL effective interest rate (EIR).\n  – >= 10% Change (Substantially Different) → EXTINGUISHMENT: Derecognize old debt, record new debt at FAIR VALUE, recognize gain/loss in P&L; lender fees capitalized, third-party costs expensed.\n  – < 10% Change (Non-Substantial Modification) → PROSPECTIVE ADJUSTMENT: NO immediate P&L gain or loss! Adjust effective interest rate prospectively; lender fees deferred, third-party costs expensed.\n• Troubled Debt Restructuring — Debtor (ASC 470-60): If debtor is in financial difficulty and lender grants a concession, recognize gain ONLY if total UNDISCOUNTED future cash flows < carrying amount.","seq":2},{"id":1000883,"no":"ASC 470 vs IAS 32 / IFRS 9","heading":"Key Cross-Framework Divergences (Convertibles & Non-Substantial Modifications)","text":"• Convertible Bonds: IAS 32 ALWAYS bifurcates compound convertible debt into Liability + Equity (creating higher effective interest expense); ASU 2020-06 keeps most US GAAP convertibles as a single liability with lower GAAP interest expense.\n• Non-Substantial Modifications (<10%): IFRS 9.5.4.3 requires an IMMEDIATE P&L modification gain/loss (recalculating PV at original EIR); ASC 470-50 amortizes the difference prospectively with NO Day-1 P&L impact.\n• Post-Year-End Refinancing / Waivers: Allowed to keep debt noncurrent under ASC 470-10 if completed before issuance; IAS 1 requires completion on or before the reporting date.","seq":3},{"id":1000884,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official FASB Codification & Reference Entry","text":"Official Portal (FASB Accounting Standards Codification®): https://asc.fasb.org/\nStatus: in_force (FASB ASC free Basic View available at https://asc.fasb.org/).","seq":4}]});
